GBTRACE canary diagnostics (schema v1)

Enable only custom_components.tuya_local.trace at DEBUG for a future canary.
The existing integration and TinyTuya debug loggers are outside this schema.
No deployment, restart, reload or logger configuration is performed by these tools.

Every message is GBTRACE followed by one JSON object. The exact envelope and
event-specific required fields are in gateway_trace_schema.json. Durations and
ts_ms use monotonic milliseconds. seq orders records within a process. run_id
is a random process token; all other IDs/slots are increasing counters. None
is allowed for gateway_slot/child_slot before acquisition or after unregister.
No identifier, name, address, DP ID/value, payload, key or exception text is
allowed in the trace. The emitter filters fields and catches sink failures.

Logical commands keep cmd_id through retries and across the worker boundary.
Request retries without cmd_id belong to polling/setup and are not counted as
logical outbound command retries by the analyzer.
call_id identifies an individual broker operation, lock_id one acquisition,
poll_id one safety poll, batch_id one nonempty drain, and push_id one received
message. pending_generation identifies one pending insertion batch; multiple
DPs in that batch share a generation. Replacements always get a fresh one.
pending_cmd_id records the original pending owner if an older cancelled
request's unsent state is included in a later command's snapshot.

mark_sent observes the unchanged production acceptance predicate. A rejected
old snapshot emits stale_mark_sent_ignored under its original cmd_id/generation.
push_ack is emitted only for matching gateway pushes, not status-poll data.
remaining_pending_count counts the still-observed pending properties belonging
to that logical command, not every child or every command on the gateway.
ack_latency_ms starts when the successful TinyTuya call returned. FAKE_IT_TIMEOUT
is unchanged: pending_expire/ack_timeout occur when normal pending-state access
actually removes expired sent state, not on a new background timer.

Health snapshots piggyback on broker activity at most once per 60 seconds,
plus a final snapshot after joining the worker. If the worker is blocked inside
TinyTuya, it cannot schedule another snapshot until it returns. Snapshot fields
are observations, not an atomic transaction across both threads. The analyzer
reports the final OBSERVED queue/pending counts, or null if not observed.

Worker blocking phases (additive schema v1 events):
  connect_start / connect_done: only the existing _get_socket(False) attempt,
    after the no-socket and reconnect-backoff checks have permitted it.
  heartbeat_start / heartbeat_done: only an actually due heartbeat(nowait=True).
  receive_start / receive_done: only parent.receive() after a readable select.
Each pair has gateway_slot and a process-local phase_id. The done event adds
duration_ms, outcome (ok/error), and socket_present, sampled before the existing
error handling/disconnect. Exceptions and truthy Err results count as errors;
connect requires True plus a present socket. A receive/heartbeat returning None
is an ok RETURN, not proof of a response, timeout absence or device success.
No result, payload or exception text is included. Phase timing is monotonic and
includes the small tracing overhead; a blocked log sink can itself add latency.
TinyTuya work inside queued status/control remains in broker_execute intervals;
these new pairs do not separately label internal connects/retries inside them.

select_slow is emitted only when the observed select exceeds BOTH 250 ms and
twice the configured select timeout. It includes start_ts_ms, duration_ms and
socket_present, including on select exceptions. Normal 100 ms timeouts are quiet.
The threshold only gates diagnostics: no timeout, retry, backoff, heartbeat,
poll cadence, lock, socket method call, sleep, await or worker wakeup is added
or changed by these observations. Diagnostic sink exceptions remain contained.

The first traced health includes available finite, nonnegative configuration
attributes: socket_timeout_ms (parent.connection_timeout), socket_retry_limit
(parent.socketRetryLimit), socket_retry_delay_ms (parent.socketRetryDelay),
heartbeat_interval_ms and select_timeout_ms. Values are read from attributes,
after the worker's existing setup; no TinyTuya or socket getter is called.
Unknown, nonnumeric or invalid values are omitted. This is a configured-value
snapshot, not an OS socket query, and is not repeated at every health event.

Usage (standard library only; keep the JSON schema beside the analyzer):
  python tools/analyze_gateway_trace.py saved-ha.log
  python tools/analyze_gateway_trace.py - < saved-ha.log

Output is deterministic JSON. Exit codes are PASS=0, WARN=1, FAIL=2. Only
GBTRACE JSON is parsed; malformed/foreign lines and fields are not echoed.
Ordinary ha core logs output may be supplied directly: complete ANSI CSI colors
and OSC terminal sequences are removed before matching. Incomplete escapes are
not repaired, and the same JSON/schema validation still applies.
Latency distributions are milliseconds with linearly interpolated percentiles.
Polling's short window is one second, separately per process and gateway.
Commands.acknowledged counts commands whose observed remaining count reached
zero; push.acknowledged counts individual matched updates. Commands.ack_timeouts
counts logical commands with at least one expired update.

worker_phases reports connect/heartbeat/receive count (starts), completed,
errors and max_duration_ms; select_slow has count and max_duration_ms. With no
observations, count is zero and maximum is null, not a claim no such work ran.
configuration_snapshots preserves the safe runtime values and their seq/time.
intervals contains paired phase spans, slow selects and broker executions with
run/gateway, IDs, seq and monotonic endpoints. An open phase has a null end and
is a strict unfinished_worker_phase FAIL; a malformed pair also fails. An error
return is worker_phase_error WARN, separately from structural integrity failures.

queue_wait_attribution lists each observed enqueue-to-execute interval, its
overlapping completed worker spans and overlap_ms clipped to that wait. The
union of those spans gives observed_phase_ms; the remainder is unattributed_ms.
It never joins different gateways/runs, guesses a phase from a five-second wait,
or fills in the end of an open phase. Normal selects, logging, scheduling gaps
and other unobserved work can remain unattributed. Event-boundary spans include
instrumentation overhead; duration_ms and event timestamp differences can differ
slightly. These fields show temporal occupancy, not the packet-level root cause.

The analyzer detects missing command stages, unreleased locks, unresolved
broker operations, missing send results, sends without intervening retries,
unfinished batches/polls, bad lifecycle ordering, worker failure, and a rising
nondecreasing backlog in the last three health observations. Missing ACK alone,
ACK timeout, error completion or cancellation are WARN, never proof of failure.
Live gateways need not stop before a capture ends; started commands, calls,
lock acquisitions, polls and drain batches must finish for a clean result.
Analysis remains strict; there is no live-capture exemption or age threshold.
An incomplete chain at EOF means the capture cannot establish completion, not
that a runtime leak has been proved. For a lifecycle PASS, select a capture
window containing complete lifecycles through a quiescent boundary. This does
not require stopping the gateway. If no such boundary exists, retain the FAIL
and inspect each open chain's first/last seq, timestamps and age at capture end.
Do not discard old unresolved operations just because newer work is active.
Blocking TinyTuya operations have no trace-enforced deadline, so recency alone
cannot safely distinguish a slow operation from a leak. A final enqueue can be
unobserved work at EOF while concurrent polls have already waited a long time.
Final queue/pending fields are the last eligible observations, not an EOF census:
final_queue_depth uses health/drain-done, final_pending_futures uses health.
Inspect later enqueues too. A worker-side execute-done does not itself prove that
HA has processed the result callback and removed that future from _pending.

Limits: capture from before the relevant lifecycles through their completion.
Truncation, runtime logging changes or dropped log records can resemble missing
events. A sink failure is deliberately swallowed to preserve device behavior.
Logging consumes CPU and the configured log sink may add I/O latency. The
analyzer cannot certify a physical action, correlate a matching unsolicited
state change with causality, see TinyTuya-internal packet retransmissions, or
promise hardware latency. A 'send' is a TinyTuya control invocation; reconnect
events observe the broker's connection path, not every internal TinyTuya action.

The virtual tests model opaque blocking status calls, unlike TinyTuya's richer
wrong-CID buffering. Their starvation bound is one select wait plus all remaining
serialized status work in the finite burst, not a hardware latency SLA.
